<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01UB">Example <span data-tag="01UB">5.1.4.2</span>.</a> Let $q: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor between ordinary categories. Then $q$ is a cartesian fibration (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="01RN" href="/tag/01RN">5.0.0.3</a>) if and only if the induced morphism of simplicial sets $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(q): \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{D}})$ is a cartesian fibration (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="01UA" href="/tag/01UA">5.1.4.1</a>). Similarly, $q$ is a cocartesian fibration if and only if $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(q)$ is a cocartesian fibration of simplicial sets. See Corollary <a data-tag="01TN" href="/tag/01TN">5.1.2.2</a>. </p>
